seg-fbe56ff7f1ed = <c1/> prints, and <c2/> publishes, a JSON state document and Home Assistant MQTT discovery messages. Discovery (retained, under <c3/>) makes three entities: trust score, trust band (with the reasons as attributes) and an <c4/> problem sensor. The state document goes to <c5/> (default <c6/>, retained): <c7/>, <c8/> (null while none), <c9/>, <c10/>, <c11/>, <c12/>, <c13/> and the advisory statement; the position is never included. Options: <c14/>, <c15/>, <c16/>.

# text
# c1: format = "mqtt"
# c2: topic<TAB>payload
seg-252025356c0e = The publisher speaks MQTT { "3.1.1" } at QoS { "0" } and reads only the broker's CONNACK. It has no credentials and no TLS, so use a broker on the local network (or a local relay that adds them). Delivery runs on its own thread behind a queue of { "1024" }: a dead broker drops messages, counted and reported at the end, and never stalls the stream. An NTP appliance or any other consumer that wants plain JSON can read the same state line. Nothing is written to a receiver or a clock. Python, WebAssembly and MCP take <c1/> and return the same <c2/> lines (nothing is published there).

